      "sequence_position": 1,
      "problem": "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{e}^{3x}}+1)=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${e}^{3x}≫{x}^{2}$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{e}^{3x}}=0\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{e}^{3x}}+1) & =0+1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
